Let’s break down clearly how Elasticsearch actually stores and retrieves data 👇

⚙️ Elasticsearch — How It Stores Values Internally
🧩 1. Basic Concept

Elasticsearch (ES) is built on Apache Lucene, a search engine library written in Java.

It stores data in inverted indexes — not like traditional databases.

So instead of rows & columns (like SQL), ES stores data as:

Documents inside Indexes

Each Index ≈ a Database Table
Each Document ≈ a Row (JSON object)
Each Field ≈ a Column

🧠 2. Storage Flow — Step by Step

Let’s say you index this JSON:

{
  "id": 1,
  "title": "Elasticsearch stores data efficiently",
  "tags": ["search", "database", "lucene"]
}


Here’s what happens inside:

🔹 Step 1: JSON → Lucene Document

Elasticsearch converts this JSON into multiple Lucene fields.

Field	Value	Type
id	1	numeric
title	"Elasticsearch stores data efficiently"	text
tags	["search", "database", "lucene"]	keyword
🔹 Step 2: Tokenization (for text fields)

Lucene analyzes text (tokenizes, lowercases, removes stop words).

For example, title → tokens:

["elasticsearch", "store", "data", "efficiently"]


These tokens are then indexed using inverted index.

🔹 Step 3: Inverted Index Structure

👉 Think of it as a word → list of documents mapping.

Term	Document IDs
"elasticsearch"	[1]
"store"	[1]
"data"	[1]
"efficiently"	[1]
"database"	[1]
"search"	[1]
"lucene"	[1]

So searching for "data" instantly finds document 1 — no scanning needed!

🔹 Step 4: Columnar Storage (Doc Values)

For fields like id, price, or date — ES stores doc values.

Doc values = column-oriented storage (like columnar databases).

Purpose:

Fast sorting, aggregations, group-by queries.

Example:

id: [1, 2, 3, 4]
price: [10, 12, 15, 20]


This is column-based (efficient for aggregations).

🔹 Step 5: Segments

Lucene writes data in immutable segments (like mini indexes).

Each index shard = multiple segments.

When new documents come:

They are written to new segments in memory

Periodically flushed to disk

Old segments get merged for optimization (merge policy)

So:

Index → Shards → Segments → Lucene inverted indexes

🔹 Step 6: Storage on Disk

Each segment has:

.tim files → term dictionary (words)

.doc files → postings list (doc IDs)

.fdt → stored fields (original JSON)

.dvd / .dvm → doc values

.fdx → field index metadata

All compressed and indexed.

🧮 Example Search Flow

Query: "data"

Analyzer tokenizes query → "data"

Lucene looks up "data" in term dictionary

Finds posting list → doc IDs [1]

Retrieves original stored fields from segment files

Ranks results using TF-IDF / BM25 scoring

💾 Storage Summary
Type of Field	Stored As	Purpose
Text	Inverted Index	Full-text search
Keyword	Hash + Doc Values	Exact match & aggregations
Numeric / Date	Doc Values (columnar)	Sorting & aggregations
Original JSON	Stored Fields	Return original document
🚀 Optimization Notes

Lucene segments are immutable → no in-place updates → new segment gets created.

Deletes are logical (mark as deleted).

Merging combines small segments into larger ones for efficiency.

Compression and columnar layout make queries extremely fast.

🧱 In Short:

Elasticsearch stores values in 2 main ways:

Inverted Index → for fast text search

Doc Values (columnar) → for sorting & aggregations